{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Entreat YHWH.</b> One needs to know why he did not turn and come to his house in this plague as he did in the plague of blood — did his magicians not show him this one too, for they did it as well? Indeed, his own mouth answered him with the reason for his agitation in this one and in all similar cases: he was shaken and called Moses to pray on his behalf, for he would react only when terrors of death fell upon him." "And this is the order of His plagues: first, the plague of blood — there was no danger of death in it; and for drinking water they found for him around the Nile, or he would acquire from Israel. Therefore he was not shaken by it." "The plague of frogs contained two things: one — the disturbance of sound, and the second — that they would enter their innards, as it is written (Exodus 7:29) ״וּבְכָה וּבְעַמְּךָ יַעֲלוּ הַצְפַרְדְּעִים״, and this plague terrifies the soul and no person is secure in his life. Therefore Pharaoh said to Moses etc. <b>and remove the frogs from me</b> — meaning, from within his innards etc." "The plague of lice — for although he suffered, there is no danger of death there as with the frogs, which would pierce his innards etc. and render him a treifah. And therefore, although he knew that it was the finger of Elohim and the magicians testified that they were unable to do it, nevertheless ״וַיֶּחֱזַק לֵב״ etc." "The plague of wild beasts — the terror of teeth of beasts that YHWH sent upon him terrified him and he feared exceedingly, and immediately he sent after them and said (Exodus 8:21) ״לְכוּ זִבְחוּ וְגוֹ׳ אָנֹכִי אֲשַׁלַּח וְגוֹ׳ הַעְתִּירוּ בַּעֲדִי״." "The plague of pestilence — since it was only on the livestock, even though he sent and looked at the livestock of Israel and not one had died, even so he did not set his heart to seek mercy." "The plague of boils — since there was no danger in it, it is also possible that he was somewhat consoled in this plague from the fact that HaShem did not explicitly mention that he too had boils, and it only said (Exodus 9:11) כִּי הָיָה הַשְּׁחִין בַּחַרְטֻמִּים וּבְכָל מִצְרָיִם — therefore he did not ask Moses to pray on his behalf." "The plague of hail — since HaShem thundered from heaven and gave forth sounds and fire fell upon them from the heavens, terror and dread fell upon them, that it would do to them like the overturning of Sodom and Gomorrah, and וּפָחַד וְרָהַב לְבָבוֹ (Isaiah 60:5), and he said ״HaShem is the righteous one, etc. Entreat HaShem, etc.״ (Exodus 9:27)." "The plague of locusts — his own mouth testifies to it, for what stirred him was because he feared the fear of death, as it is written (Exodus 10:17): ״וְיָסֵר מֵעָלַי רַק אֶת הַמָּוֶת הַזֶּה״." "The plague of darkness — we do not find that he asked Moses to pray. And perhaps it is because during the first three days he would light lamps and great lanterns and illuminate the darkness, and during the second three days, when the darkness was tangible and no person rose from beneath himself, there was no possibility for a man to go call Moses, for no person rose from beneath himself. And after the seven days of darkness were completed (Exodus Rabbah 14), immediately and at once he sent for Moses and said to him that they may go — only that they leave a pledge from them that they would return, and Moses did not accept. And since the plague had already ceased, there was no need to say to him, pray."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:03.914455541Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}